WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:00.680
Now,

1
00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.849
when an incident occurs on your watch,

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.949
resolving the immediate physical situation is only the first step.

3
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:10.409
Until it is accurately documented on the permanent record,

4
00:00:10.649 --> 00:00:14.510
that event remains an open liability for both you and the property.

5
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:15.890
To close that loop,

6
00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:18.450
we will walk through 4 specific actions,

7
00:00:18.649 --> 00:00:20.430
drafting a fact-based account,

8
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.719
submitting it into our reporting system,

9
00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:24.690
updating the daily logbook,

10
00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:26.989
and escalating the issue to leadership.

11
00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:28.190
Before we begin,

12
00:00:28.409 --> 00:00:30.469
you need 2 things ready at your post.

13
00:00:30.629 --> 00:00:34.250
Active login credentials for the incident reporting platform

14
00:00:34.669 --> 00:00:36.409
and your open daily logbook.

15
00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:41.389
Mastering this administrative routine provides the client and the company with the

16
00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:43.909
legal and operational safeguards required to

17
00:00:43.909 --> 00:00:46.450
manage site-wide security risks effectively.

18
00:00:46.869 --> 00:00:48.509
As you can see in this diagram,

19
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.849
a solid incident report is built on six essential blocks of information.

20
00:00:53.189 --> 00:00:54.509
You must collect the who,

21
00:00:54.709 --> 00:00:55.099
what,

22
00:00:55.349 --> 00:00:55.799
when,

23
00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:56.419
where,

24
00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:56.909
why,

25
00:00:56.979 --> 00:00:57.650
and how

26
00:00:58.029 --> 00:00:59.500
immediately following the event.

27
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:02.540
The most frequent error officers make at this stage

28
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.620
is letting their personal feelings bleed into the report.

29
00:01:05.919 --> 00:01:08.739
An incident report requires strict objectivity.

30
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:11.529
Look at the phrase marked with a red X on screen.

31
00:01:11.879 --> 00:01:12.239
Writing,

32
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.580
the visitor was acting sketchy is an opinion,

33
00:01:14.959 --> 00:01:17.019
and it tells the reader nothing concrete.

34
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.180
Now look at the green checkmark side.

35
00:01:19.519 --> 00:01:22.879
The visitor paced the lobby for 10 minutes and refused to show ID.

36
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.580
This version relies strictly on observable physical actions.

37
00:01:26.919 --> 00:01:27.819
When you write your draft,

38
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.589
ensure your entries are fully legible and describe the

39
00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:33.519
exact physical movements taken by all parties involved.

40
00:01:33.949 --> 00:01:36.739
Separating what you saw from how you felt protects you.

41
00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.879
If your report is scrutinized by legal counsel months later,

42
00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:41.860
clear facts stand up to questioning,

43
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:43.860
while personal opinions invite doubt.

44
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.440
With your facts gathered,

45
00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.309
step to your workstation and log into the Vigil reporting platform.

46
00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.599
On the vigil dashboard shown here,

47
00:01:51.809 --> 00:01:55.169
type your drafted facts directly into the designated text fields.

48
00:01:55.529 --> 00:01:58.230
Keep the core information exactly as you collected it.

49
00:01:58.489 --> 00:02:00.360
There is a strict deadline for this step.

50
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.199
Every incident must be entered into vigil before the end of your current shift.

51
00:02:04.449 --> 00:02:06.370
You cannot delay this until the next day.

52
00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:07.970
Once the fields are complete,

53
00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:11.380
submit the data and allow the system a moment to process the entry.

54
00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:15.380
Look at the top of the screen to locate the newly generated incident report number.

55
00:02:15.759 --> 00:02:18.250
Document this unique identifier for the next phase.

56
00:02:18.589 --> 00:02:22.259
The generated ID number links the event directly to corporate level oversight,

57
00:02:22.429 --> 00:02:25.850
ensuring the incident is archived and searchable across the organization.

58
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:26.589
Next,

59
00:02:26.669 --> 00:02:27.809
open your daily journal.

60
00:02:28.149 --> 00:02:31.550
This is officially known as the security officer report or SOR.

61
00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.429
Inside the SOR write a very brief summary

62
00:02:34.429 --> 00:02:36.350
note describing the incident that just occurred.

63
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:38.949
As shown in this digital log book,

64
00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:43.869
paste the exact vigil incident report number directly next to your brief summary.

65
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.789
If a report exists in vigil but is missing from the daily log,

66
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:50.520
it creates a dangerous information silo.

67
00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:52.889
The officer relieving you will log on

68
00:02:52.889 --> 00:02:55.289
without any immediate visibility of what happened.

69
00:02:55.699 --> 00:02:58.440
Updating the SOR connects the two systems.

70
00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.800
It guarantees the incoming shift is aware of the situation

71
00:03:01.979 --> 00:03:05.279
and never walks into an unresolved security issue blind.

72
00:03:05.619 --> 00:03:07.720
Completing the software submission is necessary,

73
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.729
but it is not enough on its own.

74
00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:14.059
Direct human notification is both a legal and operational requirement.

75
00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.679
This organizational chart shows your direct chain of command.

76
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.270
Your primary point of contact for escalation

77
00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.639
is always the site security supervisor.

78
00:03:22.889 --> 00:03:24.929
If the primary supervisor is unavailable,

79
00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.190
the chart expands to your secondary contact,

80
00:03:27.490 --> 00:03:29.589
the Performance Manager.

81
00:03:29.929 --> 00:03:32.330
This notification must happen immediately,

82
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:33.750
even after hours.

83
00:03:34.089 --> 00:03:38.350
Never wait until morning to inform leadership of a major event on the property.

84
00:03:38.729 --> 00:03:41.770
Making that direct contact is critical for your own protection.

85
00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:45.940
It transfers the ultimate liability and decision making burden from you,

86
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:47.500
the on-duty officer,

87
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:49.300
to company leadership.

88
00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:53.059
You have now completed the four milestones of proper documentation,

89
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.759
gathering objective facts,

90
00:03:54.919 --> 00:03:56.139
submitting them to vigil,

91
00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:57.880
cross-referencing your SOR

92
00:03:58.080 --> 00:03:59.910
and escalating to the chain of command.

93
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.550
By executing these steps,

94
00:04:01.639 --> 00:04:05.339
you create a permanent objective record that effectively shields the client,

95
00:04:05.679 --> 00:04:06.300
the company,

96
00:04:06.389 --> 00:04:08.479
and yourself from unnecessary liability.

97
00:04:09.039 --> 00:04:10.970
If you encounter any technical issues with

98
00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.279
the visual software interface during this process,

99
00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.410
refer directly to your standard operating

100
00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.510
procedures manual for troubleshooting steps.

101
00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.970
Following this reporting protocol ensures that every action you take

102
00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.839
on shift is backed by a permanent defensible record.

103
00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:28.890
This documentation cycle allows you to manage the full life cycle of an incident

104
00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.690
from the physical event to its final resolution.